5681], [" an", -1.3604148626327515]]}, {"t": " problem", "lp": -0.5097668766975403, "top": [[" problem", -0.5097668766975403], [" specific", -0.9818471670150757]]}, {"t": ")?", "lp": -0.0009059240110218525, "top": [[")?", -0.0009059240110218525], [")", -7.337392807006836]]}, {"t": "", "lp": -0.26620563864707947, "top": [["", -0.26620563864707947], [" That", -1.481974482536316]]}], "text": "# CCS Modes on Intel Arc (xe driver)\n\nThe `ccs_mode` parameter controls how Compute Slice (CCS) contexts are handled by the Xe driver. Here's what each mode does:\n\n## CCS Mode 1\n- **Context stealing / preemption-based**\n- CCS contexts can be scheduled on the same physical contexts as GUC-managed contexts\n- More flexible but relies on preemption behavior\n- Generally the default and most compatible option\n\n## CCS Mode 2\n- **Dedicated CCS contexts**\n- Each CCS gets its own dedicated context\n- More predictable isolation between compute contexts\n- Can be more robust on certain workloads\n\n## CCS Mode 4\n- **Explicit/forced CCS handling**\n- Typically used to force specific CCS behavior, often for debugging or when you need deterministic control over which CCS contexts are used\n\n## When to use which\n\n| Mode | Use case |\n|------|----------|\n| **1** | Default, general compatibility, most common setup |\n| **2** | When you need better isolation or hit issues with context preemption |\n| **4** | Debugging, testing, or when you need explicit control |\n\n---\n\n**Important caveat:** I'm not fully certain about the exact semantics of mode 4 versus the others, and these driver parameters can vary significantly between driver versions and kernel releases. The Xe driver has evolved quickly.\n\nFor accurate, version-specific details, I'd recommend checking:\n- The kernel source documentation (`Documentation/gpu/xe.rst` or similar)\n- The `xe` driver source in your kernel tree\n- Intel's official Arc Linux driver documentation\n\nWould you like me to elaborate on any specific mode, or are you working toward a particular use case (like a specific compute workload or troubleshooting a problem)?"}